Java data types and Sting APIs
-------------

- 8 primitive data types in Java:
	int 		32 bits
	short		16
	long		64
	(*) float	32
	double		64
	char		16
	byte		8
	boolean 1bit, size not precisely defined (possibly 1 byte)
		
	- Note: float and double have limited amount of significant digits they can store (7 - float, 16 - double). So for PI - float will be able to store only with precition of 6 fractional digits, whilst double with 15 fractional digits
	Also note, the larger the integer part, the less fractional precision you can store.
	
	- Use BigDecimal if you need to overcome this limitations
	
	- Note: you can assign integral values to float or double - value will be transferred in Scientific notation (mantissa and exponent).
	It will never overflow even with very huge numbers, but loose precision when going beyond maxim representable in mantissa (integral values > 2^24 for float and > 2^53 for double). 

	- char is only positive numbers; declared with ''; for Unicode using hexadecimal codes like '\u0634' 
	
	- literals in octal, binary and hexadecimal are written as corr-ly: 0..., 0b..., 0x...
	
	- all primitive types have the counterpart Wrapper classes
	
	- java automatically do Autoboxing and Unboxing between primitive and Wrapper types
	
	- widening and casting do not work between Wrapper classes 
	
- Widening, Narrowing, Overflow - we can cast smaller size primitive type to bigger one; we can cast bigger into smaller is value not exceeds the range; in other cases we get overflow result is unpredictable, including e.g. assign negative short value to char



