Exceptions handling
-------------

- Exception handling
	- In catch blocks you have to respect Exception class hierarchy, i.e. catching more specific exceptions must precede catching more general ones	
	- Multi-catch block is the one using syntax catch (Exception1 |Exception2 e)
		- Exceptions in multi-catch block must be disjoint (can be in hierarchy relation)
	- Just a reminder - finally block is guaranteed to always execute, no matter what (unless you call System.exit() somewhere haha)
	- With a finally present catch blocks become optional

- Try with resources: try (FileInputStream in = new FileInputStream("in.txt"); FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("out.txt")) { <code> }
	- you may have as many resources inside try as you need, separated by ; 
	- java guarantees to close all opened resources before whole construct is completed (actually rewrites it into finally under the hood)
	- Note: try with resources has catch{} and finally{} - optional if resource opening and <code> doesn't throw an exception - otherwise use catch block
	- Note: local variable declaration (of resource) within try - is not available in catch
	- Note: closing of resources is done in order reverse to opening
	- Note: resource is any class implementing AutoClosable functional interface - having method void close() 
	
- Just a reminder: Throwable is the parent of exceptions hierarchy, having Exception and Error subclasses.
- Exceptions categories in: Checked, Unchecked (Runtime is the base class)
- Exceptions to memoize:
	- RuntimeException(s)
		- ArithmeticException (divide by 0)
		- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
		- ClassCastException (casting to incompatible type)
		- IllegalArgumentException (throw yourself to signal on bad argument type e.g. negative number where must be non-negative)
		- IllegalStateException
		- NPE
		- NumberFormatException (e.g. trying to convert String into Integer)
		- Note: the majority of above Exceptions directly spring from RuntimeException, but NumberFormatException > IllegalArgumentException > RuntimeException
	- Checked Exception(s)
		(java.io)
		- IOException
		- FileNotfoundException ^
		
		(java.nio)
		- FileSystemException
		- NoSuchFileException
		- AccessDeniedException
		
	- Course: Java Fundamentals: NIO and NIO2
	
- Error - we should never catch, or try to recover
- Error(s) to memoize:
	- ExceptionInitializerError (if static class initializer fails - i.e. static variables initialization, static blocks etc)
	- StackOverflowError
	- NoClassDefFoundError (class missing at runtime, or couldn't be ClassLoaded)
	- OutOfMemoryError
	
- Note: we usually don't care for declaring any RuntimeException(s) in method signatures
- Note: Exceptions are covariant in Java, meaning in @Override method you can narrow down exception compared to type thrown in parent (overriden) method. Also you may skip throwing some exception declared by overriden method in @Override method.
- Note: Remember that "throws" clause is not part of method signature

-Exception handling Clean code principles:
	- Never catch Throwable
	- Prefer catching specific exceptions rather than general
	- Don't swallow exceptions (no empty catch blocks)






