- Java 8 Streams
	- package java.util.stream
	- stream operations split into: intermidiate & terminal
	- streams are lazy, no action will happen unless the terminal operation is present - as opposed to Collections which are eager

	- Note: stream programming is declarative, iteration is internal (not controlled by the code) as opposed to external iteration w/ Collections
	- Note: streams as functional programming don't modify it's source, but produce immutable elements at each step
	- Note: streams don't store elements, they only get them from source

	- Obtaining streams
		- Arrays.stream(array);
		- Stream.of("one", "two");
		- Stream.ofNullable("one"); // takes a single element and returns empty stream if argument is null
		- Stream.empty()
		- IntStream dice = new java.util.Random().ints(1, 7); // randoms from 1 to 6
		- try(BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file", StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
			in.lines().forEach(System.out::println); // returning a stream here
		} catch (IOException e) {
			e.printStackTrace();
		}
		- Stream.generate() - takes Supplier, generates an infinite stream
			- Stream<UUID> uuids = Stream.generate(UUID::randomUUID);
			  uuids.limit(10).forEach(System.out::println)
		- Stream.iterate(seed, UnaryOperator)
			- Stream.iterate(BitInteger.ONE, n -> n.multiply(BitInteger.TWO)); // power of 2 elements
			- Stream.iterate('A', letter -> letter <= 'Z', letter -> (char) (letter + 1)); // letters of alphabet
		- Stream builder = Stream.builder();
		  builder.add("one");
		  builder.add("two");
		  builder.add("three");

	
	- some operations on the stream: filter(), findFirst(), findAny(), anyMatch(), allMatch(), noneMatch()
		- Note: findFirst() and findAny(), anyMatch() - are examples of short-curcuiting operations, they will stop when 1-st element found

	
	- Reduction & Collection / Grouping & Partitioning
		- collect() - accepts a Collector
			- use Collectors.* factory methods to build common collectors
		- reduce((result, element) -> result.add(element)) // reduction on a stream of BigDecimal elements
		- reduce(BigDecimal::add) - same as above
		- Note: above produces Optional<>

		- reduce(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add) - same as above, with initial value
		- reduce(U identity, BiFunction<U, ? super T, U> accumulator, BinaryOperator<U> combiner); - here T is type elements of original stream; identity is initial result value; accumulator is reducer; combiner used to join independent results produced by separate streams with parallel processing.
			- Note: reduce() is designed for immutable reduction

		- Collectors.collect(): <R> R collect(Supplier<R>, BiConsumer<R, ? super T> accumulator, BiConsumer<R, R> combiner) - supplier is the one producing container of the result; accumulator is function accumulating stream elements into container; combiner is function knowing how to merge 2 intermediate result containers into one
			- Note: collect() is designed for mutable reduction (as it is BiConsumer - so it takes next element and result and updates the result - not returning new value); same is for combiner function - it is BiConsumer, so effectively taking 1-st R argument and "adding" elements into it from 2-nd R

		- Collectors.toMap(): Collector<T, ?, Map<K, U>> toMap(Function(? super T, ? extends K) keyMapper, Function<? super T, ? extends U> valueMapper, BinaryOperator<U> mergeFunction)
			- Note: keyMapper is a function to extract key from stream elements; valueMapper is a function to extract value from stream elements; mergeFunction - is how you do merging of values with the same key to get ultimate result to store in map under given 
			key. Example:
				Map<Category, BigDecimal> totalPerCategory = products().stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(Product::key, Product::value, BigDecimal::add));

				- Note: same as above can be done using grouping (see below):
				Map<Category, BigDecimal> totalPerCategory = products().stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Product::getCategory, Collectors.mapping(Product::getPrice, Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add)))

		- Collectors.groupingBy examples:
			Note: groupingBy collectors always divides original stream into number of streams using a classifier function
			- List<Product> productByCategory = products().stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Product::getCategory));
			
			- Map<Category, List<String>> productNamesByCategory = products().stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Product::getCategory, Collectors.mapping(Product::getName, Collectors::toList()))); 
			Here we're using cascading groupingBy collector method, grouping element by classification function 1-st argument, and then performing reduction operation on the values associated with each key using specified downstream Collector.

		- Other Collectors. : mapping(), reducing() 

		- Collectors.partitioningBy: partitioning is a special case of grouping. Here we partition into cheap and expensive (true and false) partitions:
			BigDecimal priceLimit = new BigDecimal("5.05");
			Map<Boolean, List<Product>> partitionedProducts = products().stream().collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(product -> product.getPrice().compareTo(priceLimit) < 0));
			- Note: partitioningBy is fundamentally binary, it always produces Map<Boolean, ...>
			- Note: here elements are split into 2 Streams, which are being collected. You can specify a downstream collector as in groupingBy, which will be collecting this streams. If no downstream collector specified, streams will be collected with toList() collector

	- Parallel streams
		- use parallelStream() instead of stream()
		- for grouping use Collectors.groupingByConcurrent() - note this collector is not preserving or guaranteeing the order of elements as they come from stream


