	- One interesting class for implementing running tasks in parallel taking first available result - ExecutorCompletionService
		- so submitting tasks into ExecutorCompletionService will make it put resulting Future into blocking queue ONLY when task completed; you can poll() the queue thus getting first ready result (or all in order of completion). Here an examplery snippet. 

		- Note: Future returned from poll() has hard guarantee of non blocking on get(), as result already there

		ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
		ExecutorCompletionService<Flight> ecs =
				new ExecutorCompletionService<>(pool);

		List<Future<Flight>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

		try {
			for (TravelAgency agency : agencies) {
				futures.add(
					ecs.submit(() -> agency.search(user, location))
				);
			}

			Flight result = null;

			for (int i = 0; i < agencies.size(); i++) {
				Future<Flight> f = ecs.poll(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
				if (f == null) {
					break; // timeout
				}
				try {
					result = f.get(); // success
					break;
				} catch (ExecutionException e) {
					// failed task → ignore and continue
				}
			}

			if (result == null) {
				throw new TimeoutException("No successful flight found");
			}

			return result;

		} finally {
			// 🔥 Cancel everything else
			futures.forEach(f -> f.cancel(true));

			// 🔥 Interrupt running threads
			pool.shutdownNow();
		}




Secure coding practices with Java
-------------


Note: courses on Pluralsight: 1) Securing Java Web Applications 2) Spring Security 3) Securing Java Application Data

- Basic principle: don't secure what is not being published. Meaning most conservatively you fully close smth and don't care for ensuring security of this e.g. private field w/o getter.

- Guidelines
	- Keep your code simple! KISS - Keep It Simple Stupid
		- C.A.R. Hoare once famously quipped: There are two ways of constructing software design: one is to make it so simple that there are obviously no deficiencies, and the other way is to make it so complicated that thare are no obvious deficiencies
	- Avoid duplication (Single responsibility)
	- Minimize permission checks
	- Document security
	- Secure 3-rd party code

	- use acess modifiers, setters - i.e. incapsulation
	- search for invariants
	- enforce checks in the constructor
	- prefer to check for ALLOWED rather than NOTE ALLOWED

	- Immutability
		- consider using final on class (or more narrowly on method) - to protect method overriding e.g.
		- make constructor private in favor of static factory methods
		- consider final on fields (also makes code thread-safe)
		- if you need to do immutable a object (container) - copy it in constructor and don't set object field to a passed parameter in constructor
			- Note: also for getters of immutable object - don't return the object itself, but rather a derivative of it e.g. a copy of object, an iterator if it's a Collection or so - return Collections.umbodifiableList(this.field)
		
		- Note: don't use Cloneable - it's way too banned pattern; it bypasses constructor (no invariants secured) apart from all other evils. (also remember Cloneable creates shallow objects by default)
			- use copy constructors if you need Cloneable - instead

		- Note: be careful with Serializable as well, cuz serialized object may be actually hacked to change values - again bypassing invariants ensurance code
			- be careful deserializing untrusted payloads
			- override readResolve() method to enforce checking - call constructor and return it's result
			- to opt out serialization in Serializable class - override readObject() and writeObject() to throw NoSerializableException
			- you can also fine grain wchich classes can use serializaction -  ObjectInputFilter.Config.setSerialFilter()

		
	- Passwords & Sensitive data
		- passwords are usually hashed to the same length, using some kind of hashing algo like Argon2(best), bcrypt
			- Note passwords must always be used with hashing only - not encrypt/decrypt, so one way hashing only!
			- Note: modern password hashing standards alwasy include salt > to prevent same passwords have same hashes > to avoid hackers use rainbow tables (tables with precomputerst password - hash values), which can be reused across many companies

		- Sensitive data categorization (shouldn't be logged ever - is you still need to log, hash this info):
			- PII (Persanally Identifiable Information), PCI (Payment Card Information), PFI (Personal Financial Information), PHI (Protected Health Information)
			- passwords, tokens, session IDs

		- Note: sensitive info can exist in memory within variables and objects - there is some risk of making thread dump will leak this information out
			- if some sensitive info still need to reside in memory, it should be hashed, encrypted or indirectly referenced

		- Avoid to show Exception fully or partially to user e.g. in UI.
			- replace untrusted exception messages (from libs)

		- To not serialize sensitive fiedls use "transient"
			- otherwise use ObjectStreamField[] array to point which fields to serialize (blocklists)

		- Protect from SQL injection attacks by usin PreparedStatement and using bind variables (! bind vars are critical)

		- Inclusion is attack of including references to malicious files or endpoints or resources
			- Don't load resources that you don't need or not recognize
			- Load only local files from known paths | check through allow list
			- Load remote files only over HTTPS 

		- Defending from DDOS
			- Define and enforce limits when processing resources (ZIP bombs, XML bombs - uncompressing melts down memory)
				- Note: recipe always use small buffers to read/transfer data - not reading it full into memory
				- Note: apply limits on max size of data (when uncompressing count how much have you read)
				- use library Apache POI ZipArchiveThresholdInputStream
			- Protect against overflow
			- Keep cleaning-up resources for GC - remove references and close opened resources (same as to avoid memory leaks)
			- Limit internal resources
				- Limit Cache sizes, longevity, eviction strategy
				- Note: example of attack on cache - making HashMap to save all values with the same key - slowdown cache lookup
				
		- SecurityManager - is to lock down risky permissions at JVM level e.g. opening resources, using class loader, reflection
			- Note: by default SecurityManager is off; when off it will be using default policy file
			- it has policy files specification as configuration (working as allow list)
				- policies can be applied to JVM modules
			- to enable SecurityManager -Djava.security.manager -Djava.security.policy=my.policy



Localization in Java
-------------

- Locale - represents specific region
	- this implements semantics of language tags 
	- Fields of Locale:
		- language ISO 639 alpha 2/3 language code e.g. "en"
		- script ISO 15924 alpha 4 script code e.g. "Latn"
		- country ISO 3166 alpha 2 country code e.g. "US"
		- variant e.g. "Win"
		- extensions key-value mappings, e.g. "u" > "ca-japanese" (Japanese calendar)

		- use displayLocaleAttributes() to see fields
		
	- methods of Locale: getXXX(), getDisplayXXX(), getISOXXX(), get ISO3XXX()
	- Locale defines constants like Country, Language as instances of Locale

	- Locale implements Serializable - writeObject() readObject() readResolve()

	- Note: Locale also has Locale.Builder - builder validates value for passed variables - IlllformedLocaleException

	- You can get default locale via: Locale.getDevault() - depending on your location

- ResourceBundle class
	- store key-value pairs which are shared for different locales, and specific attributes of target local on top
	- when needed locale-specific object, you can load ResourceBundle by ResourceBundle.getBundle(<base_name of ResourceBundles>, <current_locale>)
	- ResourceBundle is backed by properties file
	- when localizing, need to create locale specific .properties overriding default properties e.g. TextBundle.properties (default), TextBundle_fr.properties, etc. 
		- the keys remain the same across all .properties files
	
- Note: locales and bundles supported in Oracle Application Development Framework (ADF) - Faces components

- Bi-directional text - e.g. languages Arabic, Persian, Yiddish
	- directionalify (left-to-right, right-to-left)
		- is about direction of text reading, as well as alignment - note LTR > RTL alignment mirrors (except number and untranslated text)

